    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Coalbed fires are uncontrolled subsurface fires that occur around the world. These fires are believed to be significant contributors to annual CO2 emissions, and it can account for as much as 2 – 3% of total global emissions. Although many of these fires have been burning for decades, researchers have only recently begun to investigate this phenomenon. One aspect of the coalbed fire that is poorly characterized is the relationship between fires and surface fissures. Above many coalbed fire sites, long, wide fissures are observed at the surface. These are believed to be inlets and outlets of O2 and combustion exhaust gases, respectively. In some of the coalbed fire simulation models available today, these fissures are treated as fixed boundary conditions. We argue, using data collected from a coalbed fire in Durango, CO., that preexisting joints open up to form fissures only when the coalseam below burns and collapses under the overburden pressure. Using simple numerical simulations, we show preliminary results that relate the combustion front location and the location of fissure formation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
